// std/runtime/scheduler: virtual threads, and the scheduler that runs them.
//
// A virtual thread runs a function on a stack of its own. A thread runs until
// it parks, sleeps or ends; the scheduler then runs the ready threads in the
// order they became ready. With none ready, it waits for the nearest sleep to
// end; with no sleep either, every thread waits on another, and the program
// stops on a deadlock.
//
// A thread is named by an `i64` handle. Once its thread has ended, a handle
// names nothing, and waking or cancelling it does nothing. When `main` returns,
// the threads still running are cancelled, stderr names them, and the exit code
// is not zero.
//
// The runtime serves these, and under the VM the host serves them with its
// own: a program and the VM that runs it share one scheduler. This module
// imports nothing, so it can sit under `std/io` and `std/time`.

module "std/runtime/scheduler"

/// Start a thread that runs `entry`. It is ready at once, and runs once the
/// running thread parks; when `entry` returns, the thread ends.
///
/// @param entry  what the thread runs.
/// @return       the thread's handle.
@intrinsic
export spawn_thread :: fn(entry: fn() -> void) -> i64

/// Park the running thread until a `wake` names it.
///
/// A wake meant for an earlier wait can end a park too, so a thread parks in a
/// loop that checks what it waits for.
///
/// @error  stops the program when no thread is ready and none sleeps: nothing
///         would ever wake one. Also when C is running under the park: a
///         callback that C called cannot park, since another thread would run
///         over the C frames.
@intrinsic
export park :: fn() -> void

/// Make `thread` ready, unless it has ended: a parked thread runs again in its
/// turn. A thread that wakes itself and then parks lets the ready threads run
/// first.
///
/// @param thread  the handle of the thread to wake.
@intrinsic
export wake :: fn(thread: i64) -> void

/// Park the running thread for at least `nanos` nanoseconds, while the other
/// threads run. A zero or negative `nanos` returns at once; a `wake` does not
/// end the sleep.
///
/// Under `VADER_ASYNC_VIRTUAL_CLOCK`, the clock counts the sleeps alone: with
/// no thread ready, it jumps to the end of the nearest sleep.
///
/// @param nanos  how long to sleep.
/// @error        as `park`.
@intrinsic
export sleep_for :: fn(nanos: i64) -> void

/// Cancel `thread`: at its next run, it runs its pending defers and ends. The
/// running thread cancels itself at once, and `cancel_thread` does not return.
/// A defer that parks parks the cancelled thread; a thread that has ended is
/// left as it is.
///
/// @param thread  the handle of the thread to cancel.
/// @error         stops the program on the main thread, which cannot be
///                cancelled.
@intrinsic
export cancel_thread :: fn(thread: i64) -> void

/// The handle of the running thread. The main thread has one from the start.
@intrinsic
export current_thread :: fn() -> i64
